N:multiplayer-game-architecture
D:Design and review a multiplayer game's networking contract before implementation. Separate topology, authority, replication, transport, lifecycle, security, observability, and verification; produce and validate multiplayer-contract.json; route engine-specific code outward.
G:multiplayer-game netcode network-architecture authoritative-server replication prediction reconciliation snapshot-interpolation lag-compensation matchmaking host-migration websocket webrtc webtransport

U[8]:
  Choose dedicated-authoritative, listen-server, relay-assisted peer, lockstep, or asynchronous topology
  Assign authority for movement, combat, inventory, economy, match state, and hidden information
  Select command, snapshot, delta, lockstep, prediction, reconciliation, interpolation, and relevance models
  Compare WebSocket, RTCDataChannel, WebTransport, or engine transports against delivery needs
  Define lobby, allocation, join, late-join, reconnect, protocol-version, finish, and shutdown states
  Design message validation, authorization, replay defense, abuse controls, and safe logging
  Build clean and impaired network tests with mechanics-derived acceptance criteria
  Validate multiplayer-contract.json before routing to engine or backend implementation

S[8]{n,step,details}:
  1,Freeze session envelope,Record pace, player range, fairness, platforms, persistence, hidden state, reconnect, late join, migration, and version assumptions
  2,Choose topology first,Authority and continuity decide the primary model; transport does not decide who owns truth
  3,Write authority matrix,For every critical domain name the writer, allowed client intent, and authority validation; never ship hidden state to unauthorized clients
  4,Select replication per class,Use only relevant command, snapshot, delta, lockstep, prediction, reconciliation, interpolation, lag-compensation, priority, and interest mechanisms
  5,Choose channels,Map reliability, ordering, freshness, size, overflow, fallback, and browser compatibility; classic WebSocket has no backpressure
  6,Define lifecycle and security,Separate matchmaking, allocation, and simulation; validate identity, authorization, schema, state, sequence, rate, size, origin, and expiry
  7,Verify under impairment,Use separate client processes and clean plus impaired conditions; test disconnect, reconnect, version mismatch, desync, and authority violations as applicable
  8,Validate contract,Run scripts/validate-contract.py on multiplayer-contract.json and route implementation to the narrowest engine, backend, CI, security, or observability skill

R[8]:
  No universal tick, snapshot, latency, bandwidth, player-count, or queue target is copied into a project without mechanics and measurement evidence
  Server authority is applied per trusted state domain; clients may still own input collection, presentation, and speculative visuals
  WebSocket is a client-server transport, RTCDataChannel is a peer data channel, and WebTransport supplies HTTP/3 streams and datagrams; none chooses authority
  Matchmaking, game-server allocation, and match simulation are separate responsibilities
  Competitive or ranked peer or listen-server topology requires explicit risk acceptance
  Hidden state not needed by a client is not replicated to that client
  Cloud provisioning, paid services, live credentials, deploys, and publication require separate approval
  The validator is Python 3.9+, standard-library only, read-only, and rejects placeholders

E[4]{type,command}:
  Install skill,npx skills add https://github.com/akillness/jeo-skills --skill multiplayer-game-architecture
  Validate example,python3 .agent-skills/multiplayer-game-architecture/scripts/validate-contract.py .agent-skills/multiplayer-game-architecture/references/contract-example.json
  Self-test validator,python3 .agent-skills/multiplayer-game-architecture/scripts/validate-contract.py --self-test
  Validate skill,bash .agent-skills/skill-standardization/scripts/validate_skill.sh .agent-skills/multiplayer-game-architecture
